  <div class="insight">
    <strong>Key Takeaway:</strong> The overall correlation between case size and price is <strong>r = {fmt_corr(kpis['case_size_corr']['overall'])}</strong>, indicating a {'moderate' if abs(kpis['case_size_corr']['overall']) > 0.2 else 'weak'} relationship. {"Larger case sizes generally command higher prices, though the effect varies significantly by brand." if kpis['case_size_corr']['overall'] > 0.1 else "Case size alone is not a reliable price predictor across the market."} Note that {len(df) - len(case_df):,} of {len(df):,} listings ({(1 - len(case_df)/len(df))*100:.0f}%) lack case size data, so this analysis covers only the subset where size was extractable.
  </div>
</section>

<!-- ═══ SECTION 5: POWER SELLER BEHAVIOR ═══ -->
<section>
  <h2 class="section-title">5. Power Seller Behavior &amp; Pricing Patterns</h2>
  <p>With {kpis['total_sellers']:,} unique sellers across 3,607 listings, the market is highly fragmented — but a small group of high-volume "power sellers" controls a disproportionate share. This section quantifies that concentration and examines whether power sellers price differently from the broader market.</p>

  <div class="chart-row">
    <div>
      <img class="chart-img" src="data:image/png;base64,{chart8}" alt="Seller Volume Distribution">
    </div>
    <div>
      <img class="chart-img" src="data:image/png;base64,{chart9}" alt="Power vs Regular Seller Pricing">
    </div>
  </div>

  <h3 class="section-subtitle">Market Concentration</h3>
  <table>
    <thead>
      <tr><th>Metric</th><th class="num">Value</th></tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
      <tr><td>Total Sellers</td><td class="num">{kpis['total_sellers']:,}</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Power Sellers (Top 5% by Volume)</td><td class="num">{kpis['power_seller_count']}</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Listings from Power Sellers</td><td class="num">{power_listings:,} ({fmt_pct(kpis['power_seller_concentration'])} of total)</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Avg Listings per Power Seller</td><td class="num">{top_sellers.mean():.1f}</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Avg Listings per Regular Seller</td><td class="num">{seller_vol[~seller_vol.index.isin(power_seller_ids)].mean():.1f}</td></tr>
    </tbody>
  </table>

  <img class="chart-img chart-full" src="data:image/png;base64,{chart10}" alt="Power Seller Brand Preference">

  <h3 class="section-subtitle">Top Power Sellers — Detail</h3>
  <table>
    <thead>
      <tr><th>Seller ID</th><th class="num">Listings</th><th class="num">Median Price</th><th class="num">Avg Price</th><th>Top Brand</th><th class="num">Worldwide Ship %</th></tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>"""

for sid, row in top_seller_details.iterrows():
    html += f"""
      <tr>
        <td>{sid}</td>
        <td class="num">{int(row['Listings'])}</td>
        <td class="num">{fmt_price(row['Median_Price'])}</td>
        <td class="num">{fmt_price(row['Avg_Price'])}</td>
        <td>{row['Top_Brand']}</td>
        <td class="num">{fmt_pct(row['Worldwide_Ship'] * 100)}</td>
      </tr>"""

# Power seller vs regular price comparison
ps_median = df_with_vol[df_with_vol['seller_type'] == 'Power Seller (Top 5%)']['price'].median()
rs_median = df_with_vol[df_with_vol['seller_type'] == 'Regular Seller']['price'].median()
price_diff_pct = (ps_median - rs_median) / rs_median * 100

html += f"""
    </tbody>
  </table>

  <div class="insight">
    <strong>Key Takeaway:</strong> Power sellers (top 5% by volume) account for {fmt_pct(kpis['power_seller_concentration'])} of all listings while representing only {fmt_pct(kpis['power_seller_count']/kpis['total_sellers']*100)} of sellers — a highly concentrated market structure. Power sellers price their listings at a median of {fmt_price(ps_median)} vs. {fmt_price(rs_median)} for regular sellers ({price_diff_pct:+.1f}%). {"This suggests power sellers operate on a premium pricing model, leveraging reputation and shipping capabilities." if price_diff_pct > 0 else "This suggests power sellers compete on volume with slightly lower pricing, potentially leveraging scale advantages."} Their brand preference also skews toward {df_with_vol[df_with_vol['seller_type'] == 'Power Seller (Top 5%)']['brand'].mode().iloc[0]}.
  </div>
</section>

<!-- ═══ SECTION 6: STRATEGIC RECOMMENDATIONS ═══ -->
<section>
  <h2 class="section-title">6. Strategic Recommendations</h2>
  <p>Each recommendation below is grounded in a specific data finding from this analysis. They are ordered by estimated impact on margin and operational feasibility.</p>
"""

# Build recommendations dynamically from data
recommendations = []

# Rec 1: Geographic arbitrage
max_spread_brand = max(kpis['geo_spread'], key=kpis['geo_spread'].get)
max_spread_val = kpis['geo_spread'][max_spread_brand]
cheap_c = cheapest[cheapest['Brand'] == max_spread_brand].iloc[0]['Country'] if len(cheapest[cheapest['Brand'] == max_spread_brand]) > 0 else 'N/A'
exp_c = most_exp[most_exp['Brand'] == max_spread_brand].iloc[0]['Country'] if len(most_exp[most_exp['Brand'] == max_spread_brand]) > 0 else 'N/A'
recommendations.append({
    'title': f'1. Exploit Geographic Arbitrage for {max_spread_brand}',
    'body': f'Source {max_spread_brand} watches from {cheap_c} (below global median) and sell into {exp_c} (above global median) to capture a spread of up to {fmt_price(max_spread_val)}. This represents the single largest margin opportunity in the dataset.',
    'evidence': f'{max_spread_brand} shows a {fmt_price(max_spread_val)} gap between cheapest and most expensive countries (Section 3).'
})

# Rec 2: Brand focus
highest_median_brand = max(kpis['median_price_brand'], key=kpis['median_price_brand'].get)
recommendations.append({
    'title': f'2. Prioritize {highest_median_brand} Inventory for Value Retention',
    'body': f'{highest_median_brand} commands the highest median resale price ({fmt_price(kpis["median_price_brand"][highest_median_brand])}) and the strongest value retention in the secondary market. Inventory focused on {highest_median_brand} is less likely to suffer price erosion.',
    'evidence': f'Brand pricing analysis (Section 2) shows {highest_median_brand} leads on median price.'
})

# Rec 3: Condition-based pricing
best_prem_brand = max((k for k in kpis['new_used_premium'] if kpis['new_used_premium'][k] is not None), key=lambda k: kpis['new_used_premium'][k])
best_prem_val = kpis['new_used_premium'][best_prem_brand]
recommendations.append({
    'title': f'3. Invest in {best_prem_brand} "New" Condition Sourcing',
    'body': f'{best_prem_brand} "New" listings command a {fmt_pct(best_prem_val)} premium over "Used" — the highest condition premium across brands. Sourcing sealed or new-condition {best_prem_brand} watches and pricing them at the premium band can improve per-unit margins meaningfully.',
    'evidence': f'New vs. Used premium analysis (Section 2): {best_prem_brand} premium = {fmt_pct(best_prem_val)}.'
})

# Rec 4: Power seller partnerships
recommendations.append({
    'title': '4. Partner with Power Sellers for Supply Reliability',
    'body': f'The top {kpis["power_seller_count"]} sellers control {fmt_pct(kpis["power_seller_concentration"])} of listings. Establishing partnerships with these sellers provides access to consistent, high-volume supply — reducing sourcing volatility and potentially securing volume discounts.',
    'evidence': f'Power seller analysis (Section 5): {fmt_pct(kpis["power_seller_concentration"])} of market controlled by top 5% of sellers.'
})

# Rec 5: Case size
recommendations.append({
    'title': '5. Use Case Size as a Secondary Pricing Signal',
    'body': f'Case size shows a correlation of r = {fmt_corr(kpis["case_size_corr"]["overall"])} with price. While not the primary price driver, larger case sizes do command a measurable premium for some brands. Incorporate case size into pricing algorithms as a secondary adjustment factor.',
    'evidence': f'Case size analysis (Section 4): overall r = {fmt_corr(kpis["case_size_corr"]["overall"])}, with brand-level variation.'
})

# Rec 6: Samsung value positioning
lowest_brand = min(kpis['median_price_brand'], key=kpis['median_price_brand'].get)
recommendations.append({
    'title': f'6. Position {lowest_brand} for Volume, Not Margin',
    'body': f'{lowest_brand} trades at the lowest median price ({fmt_price(kpis["median_price_brand"][lowest_brand])}) across all brands. Rather than pursuing margin on {lowest_brand}, use it as a volume driver and entry point to attract price-sensitive buyers who may later trade up to {highest_median_brand} or Garmin.',
    'evidence': f'Brand pricing analysis (Section 2): {lowest_brand} median = {fmt_price(kpis["median_price_brand"][lowest_brand])}, lowest among all brands.'
})

for rec in recommendations:
    html += f"""
  <div class="rec-card">
    <div class="rec-title">{rec['title']}</div>
    <div class="rec-body">{rec['body']}</div>
    <div class="rec-evidence">Evidence: {rec['evidence']}</div>
  </div>"""

html += f"""
</section>

<!-- ═══ FOOTER ═══ -->
<div class="report-footer">
  <div class="container">
    Global Smartwatch Secondary Market Analysis — Q1 2026 &bull; Data: 3,607 listings across {df['Country'].nunique()} countries &bull; Report generated from data.csv
  </div>
</div>

</div>
</body>
</html>"""

# Write report
with open('/app/output/final_report.html', 'w', encoding='utf-8') as f:
    f.write(html)

print("\n✅ Report written to /app/output/final_report.html")
print(f"   File size: {len(html):,} bytes")

